<mujocoinclude>
  <!-- ==========================================================================
       fixture.xml — steel bracket with a vertical through-bore. The arm that is
       NOT inserting holds this steady; it is a loose free body with no jig, so
       an unheld fixture just gets pushed across the bench. That is the whole
       reason the task is bimanual.

       Body frame: origin ON THE BORE AXIS, at the underside of the base plate.
       A resting fixture therefore has body z = 0 (the bench top) and the bore
       sits directly over (x, y).

       MuJoCo primitives cannot have holes, so the bore is an octagonal ring of
       8 thin boxes, each radial-x / tangential-y / vertical-z and rotated k*45
       degrees about z. The tangential half-width (8 mm) is far larger than the
       octagon's half-side (3.73 mm), so neighbours interpenetrate heavily and
       the bore cannot leak. Geoms in one body never self-collide, so all that
       overlap is free.

         bore aperture   regular octagon, inscribed radius 0.009 (18 mm across
                         flats), circumscribed 0.009742
         guided depth    z = 0.000 -> 0.026 (26 mm), 20 mm of it raised collar
         capture radius  4.0 mm, measured: a pin released within 4 mm of the
                         axis enters, at 5 mm it does not

       The bore runs CLEAR THROUGH plate and collar. Standing on the bench, the
       bench closes the bottom, so a pin bottoms out at 26 mm of travel — a
       hard, repeatable insertion stop rather than a soft one on the head.

       Colour is plain rgba on every geom, not material=. Both work under
       domain randomization in MuJoCo 3.10 (geom_rgba overrides a material's
       colour whenever it differs from the compiler default 0.5 0.5 0.5 1), but
       never write material= AND rgba= on the same geom: the compiler bakes the
       rgba into geom_rgba and permanently masks the material.

       Use quat, never axisangle, in an include file: the angle unit depends on
       the PARENT scene's <compiler angle=...>, which an include cannot see.
       ========================================================================== -->
  <worldbody>
    <!-- Home slot: bore near the midline so either arm can reach it, grip tab
         out on the left arm's side. `SceneController` jitters this per seed. -->
    <body name="fixture" pos="-0.04 -0.20 0">
      <freejoint/>

      <!-- Base plate, 80 x 50 x 6 mm, built as a frame of four boxes so the
           bore passes through it. The inner opening is +/-0.010: outside the
           aperture (whose max |x| is 0.009) and inside the ring's outer
           envelope, so there is material everywhere and no gap. At +/-0.016
           the octagon's 45-degree flats leave four corner gaps that read as a
           modelling defect from the top-down camera.

           The -x half is long: it carries the grip tab and pushes the support
           polygon out underneath it, which is what keeps the fixture from
           tipping when an arm pinches the tab. -->
      <geom name="fixture_plate_xn" type="box" size="0.020 0.025 0.003" pos="-0.030 0 0.003"
            mass="0.030" rgba="0.58 0.60 0.63 1" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="fixture_plate_xp" type="box" size="0.010 0.025 0.003" pos="0.020 0 0.003"
            mass="0.015" rgba="0.58 0.60 0.63 1" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="fixture_plate_yn" type="box" size="0.010 0.0075 0.003" pos="0 -0.0175 0.003"
            mass="0.005" rgba="0.58 0.60 0.63 1" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="fixture_plate_yp" type="box" size="0.010 0.0075 0.003" pos="0 0.0175 0.003"
            mass="0.005" rgba="0.58 0.60 0.63 1" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>

      <!-- Bore / collar. Each box spans radius 0.009 -> 0.017 and z 0 -> 0.026.

           friction 0.4, lower than the plate, so the pin does not jam in the
           bore. MuJoCo mixes friction as the elementwise MAX of the pair, so
           the SHAFT carries 0.4 as well — one low side achieves nothing. The
           gripper pads are 2.0, so the max still resolves to 2.0 there and the
           grasp is unaffected.

           solref="0.005 1" is REQUIRED; do not drop it. With the default, a
           12 mm head striking this 18 mm aperture at ~1.1 m/s punches straight
           through: it buries itself past the boxes' 13 mm half-height, the box
           contact normal FLIPS, and the collar then drives the head downward
           and out. Stiffer contact arrests it in the first millimetres so the
           normal never flips. Measured 7/7 held up to 2.8 m/s with it, 0/7
           without. Reducing the timestep does not help — this is normal-flip,
           not tunnelling. MuJoCo solmix-AVERAGES solref across a contacting
           pair, so pin.xml must carry the same value or the fix does nothing. -->
      <geom name="fixture_bore_0" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="0.013000 0.000000 0.013" quat="1 0 0 0"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_1" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="0.009192 0.009192 0.013" quat="0.9238795 0 0 0.3826834"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_2" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="0.000000 0.013000 0.013" quat="0.7071068 0 0 0.7071068"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_3" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="-0.009192 0.009192 0.013" quat="0.3826834 0 0 0.9238795"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_4" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="-0.013000 0.000000 0.013" quat="1 0 0 0"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_5" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="-0.009192 -0.009192 0.013" quat="0.9238795 0 0 0.3826834"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_6" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="0.000000 -0.013000 0.013" quat="0.7071068 0 0 0.7071068"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>
      <geom name="fixture_bore_7" type="box" size="0.004 0.008 0.013"
            pos="0.009192 -0.009192 0.013" quat="0.3826834 0 0 0.9238795"
            mass="0.006" rgba="0.66 0.68 0.71 1" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001" solref="0.005 1"/>

      <!-- Grip tab: a vertical fin at the -x end, 10 mm thick (the jaws close
           along local x and span ~30 mm), 24 mm wide, standing z 0.006 ->
           0.040. Its inner jaw face lands at x ~= -0.031, a clear 14 mm from
           the collar's outer face at x = -0.017, so the fingers never foul the
           collar while holding. -->
      <geom name="fixture_grip_tab" type="box" size="0.005 0.012 0.017" pos="-0.041 0 0.023"
            mass="0.024" rgba="0.50 0.52 0.56 1" friction="1 0.005 0.0001"/>

      <!-- THE BORE REFERENCE FRAME, and the single most load-bearing line in
           this file: `sim/task.py::pin_inserted` measures depth, lateral
           offset and tilt in this site's frame.
             position     centre of the bore ENTRANCE (top opening), z = 0.026
             orientation  quat 0 1 0 0 = 180 degrees about local x, so the
                          site's local +z points DOWN THE BORE — the direction
                          a pin travels while being inserted
           Depth is 0 at the entrance and +0.026 fully home. Because the site
           rides the body, all of it stays correct while the fixture is held in
           mid-air, which is the normal case for this task. -->
      <site name="hole_axis" pos="0 0 0.026" quat="0 1 0 0" size="0.002"
            rgba="0.1 0.9 0.3 1"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>
</mujocoinclude>
